WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:07.133
Sitting up in bed, I tried to calm myself. Maybe the sound I'd heard at my door was Pilot.

2
00:00:07.135 --> 00:00:12.800
Sometimes when the kitchen door was left open, the big dog found his way upstairs.

3
00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:18.630
In the mornings I'd often seen him lying in the corridor outside Mr. Rochester's door.

4
00:00:18.631 --> 00:00:26.949
I lay down again and tried to sleep. But I'd barely closed my eyes when I heard a demonic laugh right next to me.

5
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:36.530
The head of my bed was near the door, and at first I thought a goblin was crouching by my pillow. I rose, looked around, and saw nothing.

6
00:00:36.531 --> 00:00:43.522
Meanwhile the unnatural sound was repeated, and this time I knew it came from the other side of my door.

7
00:00:43.523 --> 00:00:49.418
My first impulse was to lock the door; my second was to cry out again, "Who's there?"

8
00:00:49.419 --> 00:00:58.759
Something gurgled and moaned. Before long I heard steps retreating down the corridor toward the third-story staircase.

9
00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:04.330
Then I heard the door at the foot of those stairs open and close, and all was still.

10
00:01:04.331 --> 00:01:12.576
"Was that Grace Poole?" I asked myself. "Is she possessed by a devil? I can't stay here by myself."

11
00:01:12.577 --> 00:01:23.395
I hurried to put on my dress and a shawl and opened my door to go in search of Mrs. Fairfax. Just outside the room, a burning candle sat on the floor.

12
00:01:23.396 --> 00:01:29.723
I was surprised by this, but I was even more amazed to find that the air was filled with smoke.

13
00:01:29.724 --> 00:01:37.476
I looked around and saw that Mr. Rochester's door was open. A cloud of smoke billowed out.

14
00:01:37.477 --> 00:01:45.127
I ran into the room. Tongues of flame darted around the bed, and the bed curtains were on fire!

15
00:01:45.128 --> 00:01:50.975
In the midst of the smoke and fire, Mr. Rochester lay motionless in a deep sleep.

16
00:01:50.976 --> 00:01:56.044
"Wake up! Wake up!" I screamed as I shook him.

17
00:01:56.045 --> 00:02:05.959
But the smoke had stupefied him. There wasn't a moment to lose because the sheets were catching fire. I rushed to his basin and pitcher.

18
00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:11.537
Fortunately one was wide and the other was deep, and they were both filled with water.

19
00:02:11.538 --> 00:02:22.189
I threw the water on the bed and its occupant. Then I ran to my room for my own pitcher, hurried back, and poured the water over Mr. Rochester.

20
00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:25.940
"Is there a flood?" he cried, waking at last.

21
00:02:25.941 --> 00:02:32.320
"No, sir, but there has been a fire," I replied. "Get up and I'll fetch you a candle."

22
00:02:32.321 --> 00:02:39.604
"Is that Jane Eyre?" he demanded. "What have you done to me? Were you plotting to drown me?"

23
00:02:39.605 --> 00:02:44.333
"Somebody has plotted something, sir!" I said.

24
00:02:44.334 --> 00:02:49.664
While he put on a dressing gown, I ran to fetch the candle from the corridor.

25
00:02:49.665 --> 00:02:56.813
He took it from my hand and surveyed the blackened bed, the drenched sheets, the soaked carpet.

26
00:02:56.814 --> 00:03:01.231
"What happened? Who did this?" he asked.

27
00:03:01.232 --> 00:03:06.992
I briefly told him what I had heard in my room and how I had extinguished the fire.

28
00:03:06.993 --> 00:03:12.228
He listened very gravely and did not immediately speak when I had concluded.

29
00:03:12.229 --> 00:03:15.741
"Shall I call Mrs. Fairfax?" I asked.

30
00:03:15.742 --> 00:03:19.804
"What can she do? Let her sleep undisturbed."

31
00:03:19.805 --> 00:03:23.382
"Then I will fetch Leah and wake John and his wife, Mary."

32
00:03:23.383 --> 00:03:35.131
"No, be still. Sit down in the armchair and place your feet on the stool to keep them dry," he said. "Are you cold? Let me help you with my cloak."

33
00:03:35.132 --> 00:03:39.382
He pulled out a long cloak and wrapped it around my shoulders.

34
00:03:39.383 --> 00:03:43.958
"I'm going to take the candle and leave you for a few minutes," he said.

35
00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:55.519
"Remain where you are until I return and be as quiet as a mouse. I must pay a visit to the third floor. Remember: don't move or call anyone."

36
00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.438
He left the room, and I heard him walk down the corridor very softly.

37
00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:06.419
Then he opened the staircase door with as little noise as possible and shut it after him.

38
00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:18.933
Left in total darkness, I listened for any noise but heard nothing. A very long time passed. I grew weary, and I was cold despite the cloak.

39
00:04:18.934 --> 00:04:26.155
I didn't see the point of staying and was on the verge of quietly returning to my room when I heard his footsteps in the corridor.

40
00:04:26.156 --> 00:04:31.843
Looking pale and very gloomy, Mr. Rochester entered the room.

41
00:04:31.844 --> 00:04:36.759
"It is as I thought," he said, setting the candle on the washstand.

42
00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.560
"What is, sir?"

43
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:44.974
Mr. Rochester made no reply but stood with his arms folded, staring at the floor.

44
00:04:44.975 --> 00:04:53.392
After a few minutes he said in a rather peculiar tone, "I forget whether you said that you'd seen anything when you opened your bedroom door."

45
00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:56.233
"I only saw the candle on the floor."

46
00:04:56.234 --> 00:05:00.740
"But you've heard that laugh before or something like it?"

47
00:05:00.741 --> 00:05:10.202
"Yes, sir. There's a woman named Grace Poole who sews here. She laughs in that way, and she's an odd person."

48
00:05:10.203 --> 00:05:21.706
"Just so . . . Grace Poole . . . you've guessed it. She is, as you say, odd. Very odd. Well, I shall think about this subject.

49
00:05:21.707 --> 00:05:29.683
Meanwhile I'm glad we're the only people who know the precise details of tonight's incident. Say nothing about it."

50
00:05:29.684 --> 00:05:31.222
"Yes, sir."

51
00:05:31.223 --> 00:05:38.589
"I'll account for this state of affairs," he said, pointing to the bed. "Now return to your own room.

52
00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:47.589
I shall do very well on the library sofa for the rest of the night. It's nearly four o'clock. In two hours the servants will be up."

53
00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.530
"Good night then, sir." I got up to leave.

54
00:05:51.531 --> 00:05:56.855
He looked surprised, which seemed odd to me as he'd just told me to go.

55
00:05:56.856 --> 00:06:02.329
"What!" he exclaimed. "Are you leaving me already and in that way?"

56
00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:05.249
"You said I might go, sir."

57
00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:13.825
"You've snatched me from a horrible and excruciating death, and you walk past me as if we were strangers! At least shake hands."

58
00:06:13.826 --> 00:06:21.755
He held out his hand and I gave him mine. He took it first in one and then in both his own hands.

59
00:06:21.755 --> 00:06:28.222
"You've saved my life. I have a pleasure in owing you so immense a debt."

60
00:06:28.223 --> 00:06:35.315
He paused and gazed at me. Words almost trembled on his lips, but he said nothing.

61
00:06:35.316 --> 00:06:37.569
"You owe me nothing, sir."

62
00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:48.458
"I knew you would do me good in some way when I first saw you. Your eyes did not strike delight in my heart for nothing. Good night, my cherished savior."

63
00:06:48.459 --> 00:06:54.042
Strange energy was in his voice, and strange fire was in his look.

64
00:06:54.043 --> 00:06:58.220
"I'm glad I happened to be awake. Good night, sir."

65
00:06:58.221 --> 00:07:07.859
I went back to bed but never thought of sleep. Too agitated to rest, I rose as soon as day dawned.

